\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  Bank Indonesia Surprises With 25-BPs Hike in Overnight Deposit Rate to 4.25%\b0\par\par\uc2 By I Made Sentana and Linda Silaen   \par \uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2013\par 02:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA--\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  has raised the interest rate of its overnight deposit facility 25 basis points to 4.25% with immediate effect, to help ease mounting pressures on the rupiah, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "ready to take any necessary step" to maintain monetary stability following the rupiah's recent depreciation, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  said in a press release.\par \par \uc2 The surprise hike in the overnight rate comes ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meeting Thursday, where analysts have said the bank is likely to hold its benchmark BI rate at 5.75% for the 16th consecutive month.\par \par \uc2 The pressures on the rupiah intensified recently as foreign investors pulled out from Indonesian bonds and stocks in anticipation of a cutback in the U.S. Federal Reserve's bond-buying program, amid signs of economic recovery there.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar traded at IDR9,830 late Tuesday among banks in Jakarta, but banks have been selling the greenback to customers above IDR10,000 since Monday. Local stocks fell 3.5% Tuesday, while the yield of government rupiah-denominated bonds rose an average 12 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  has been suspected of selling dollars in the market since last month to slow the rupiah's depreciation. The rupiah has fallen nearly 3.5% against the dollar so far this year, also due to concerns over the country's current-account deficit.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  said last week \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by about $2.3 billion last month to $105 billion, which economists said was mostly due to its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday it will continue to supply the market with dollars if needed.\par \par \uc2 The government later this month plans to raise subsidized fuel prices to discourage consumption of such fuels, which in turn will help reduce fuel imports.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Chatib Basri told reporters late Tuesday that he expects the rupiah will appreciate after the government raises subsidized fuel prices.\par \par \uc2 "The government's plan is clear that subsidized fuel prices will be increased," Mr. Basri said. "The market will welcome the policy."\par \par \uc2 Write to I Made Sentana at i-made.sentana@dowjones.com and Linda Silaen at Linda.silaen@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 12-06-13 0051GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020130612e96c0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
