\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Asian bonds sell off, \uc2 Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 964 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2013\par 11:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Fed policy view pushes 10-yr Treasury yld to 13-mnth high\par \par \uc2 * Asian bond selloff occurs ahead of 5-yr Treasury auction\par \par \uc2 * Foreigners sell record amounts of Korean bond futures\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  steps in to buy bonds as yields surge\par \par \uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, May 29 (Reuters) - A spike in U.S. interest rates triggered by expectations the Federal Reserve will tighten policy soon spurred a massive selloff in Asian bonds on Wednesday and forced \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in to support its bond market.\par \par \uc2 The selling was triggered by a drop in U.S. Treasuries during Asian trading that took U.S. 10-year yields to 13-month highs, extending the run-up in yields since Fed Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 's testimony last week.\par \par \uc2 That testimony raised the possibility of the Fed rolling back its extraordinary quantitative easing (QE) program sooner than markets had anticipated.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners who have been huge buyers of high-yielding Asian debt led the selling, traders said, putting pressure on bond markets in Indonesia, Malaysia, Philippines, South Korea and Australia.\par \par \uc2 As Australian bonds dropped, 10-year cash yields jumped to two-month highs at 3.48 percent and the Aussie dollar hit a 19-month low.\par \par \uc2 "These are the places which have seen the most fund inflows into the bond markets during the search for yield," said Nizam Idris, strategist with the \uc2 Macquarie Group\uc2  based in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "But when growth seems to be coming back and the Fed could taper or unwind QE, then these flows are reversed.\par \par \uc2 "And 10-year Treasury yields are now at levels where most of these trades are being repriced."\par \par \uc2 While the U.S. dollar gained in the past week as concerns mounted over how soon the Fed will withdraw its massive monetary stimulus, bond markets remained largely steady.\par \par \uc2 Expectations the Fed's policy tightening will be gradual and be accompanied by stronger growth had encouraged foreigners to stay invested.\par \par \uc2 The fears that yields will rise faster, thereby leading to cash outflows from emerging markets and into dollar assets, intensified after Tuesday's auction of U.S. 2-year notes met with lukewarm demand and drew a higher-than-expected yield. The Treasury department is set to auction 5-year notes on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. 10-year yields were quoted at 2.18 percent at 0900 GMT, and have risen 56 basis points since early May.\par \par \uc2 In Indonesia, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced into defending the rupiah and rare bond-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after rupiah bonds dived.\par \par \uc2 Five-year Indonesian government bond yields hit a 2-month high of 5.29 percent, taking the total climb in yields over two weeks to 0.4 percentage points, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bond buying pulled yields lower. Traders said the rupiah fell below 9,900 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  (BI) Deputy Governor Perry Warjiyo said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  "..because some short term foreign investors or traders are selling off their bonds."\par \par \uc2 "We are committed to be in the market to stabilize the exchange rate of the rupiah. Because of it, we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  both in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds," Warjiyo said.\par \par \uc2 FOREIGNERS SELL\par \par \uc2 Foreigners hold large amounts of dollar and local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated bonds in Asia. Their largest holdings as a proportion of the total bonds issued are in Indonesia and Malaysia.\par \par \uc2 Data from Indonesia's debt office showed offshore investors held 305.33 trillion rupiah ($30.8 billion) of government bonds, or 34 percent of the total outstanding as of May 24.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's holdings of rupiah government bonds increased from 1.9 trillion rupiah at end of January to 22.6 trillion rupiah on May 24.\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, foreigners' net selling of leading June 3-year bond futures hit a record 4.5 trillion won on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "This kind of foreign selling is unprecedented, so the repercussions in the coming days could be considerable," said a trader in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 Yield on the benchmark Korean 10-year government debt rose to its highest level in more than three months, while the won fell half a percent against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso hit a 9-month low.\par \par \uc2 In Malaysia, 5-year yields hit an 8-month high of 3.3 percent. Yields on Thailand's 5-year government bonds were at a one-month high of 3 percent despite \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  cutting policy rates on Wednesday to support the economy and deter hot money inflows.\par \par \uc2 Stock markets weakened, with Hong Kong's shares faring the worst.\par \par \uc2 Yields on dollar-denominated Indonesian and Philippine credits, which are most vulnerable to U.S. Fed policy because they are priced and quoted at a spread to \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, also rose on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine 2037 bond was quoted at 112 cents and Indonesia's 2043 bond at 93 cents per dollar of bond value, both down four to five cents in the past week. The Philippine peso hit a 9-month low.\par \par \uc2 "There is now the view that the U.S. recovery story is real and sustainable," said Macquarie's Idris.\par \par \uc2 Ten-year U.S. yields would have to climb above 3 percent to reach 'escape velocity' or a level that would imply a sustainable economic recovery and therefore higher yields, Idris said, adding he expected markets would only get a green light signal if U.S. jobs data next week was really strong.\par \par \uc2 "I am riding this move but only cautiously," he said. (Additional reporting by Cecile Lefort in Sydney, Vincent Se Young Lee in Seoul, Rieka Rahadiana and Adriana Nina Kusuma in Jakarta, Umesh Desai in Hong Kong; Editing by \uc2 Simon Cameron-Moore\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA (WRAPUP 1) | ABX | ABN | D | E | RBN | IF | M | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130529e95t000d0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
