\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 3-\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  keeps rate at 5.75 pct, cautious on growth\b0\par\par\uc2 984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2013\par 09:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, April 11 (Reuters) - \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its benchmark rate unchanged at a record low 5.75 percent on Thursday, as expected, as it sees inflationary pressures easing and is concerned about slowing economic growth.\par \par \uc2 All 14 analysts polled by Reuters had forecast the rate would be left unchanged, as core inflation eased in March and the government has not made any move yet to cut fuel subsidies.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  (BI) sounded more cautious on global economic growth prospects than in previous statements, leading some economists to see it keeping rates on hold all year. BI has kept the policy rate unchanged since February 2012, when it was cut by 25 basis points.\par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS\par \par \uc2 - BI says rate consistent with inflation targets\par \par \uc2 - BI says sees inflationary pressures easing\par \par \uc2 - BI sees slower 2013 economic growth at 6.2-6.6 pct\par \par \uc2 - BI sees moderate depreciation pressure on rupiah\par \par \uc2 Graphic for Indonesia inflation, rates:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/nef95s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/nef95s}}}\par \par \uc2 COMMENTS:\par \par \uc2 HAK BIN CHUA, ECONOMIST, BANK OF AMERICA MERRIL LYNCH, SINGAPORE\par \par \uc2 "Soft commodity prices are starting to slow overall GDP growth. The prospect of higher energy costs if the government decides to ration or adjust subsidized fuel prices may cut growth by another notch.\par \par \uc2 "The risk of monetary tightening, either via FASBI or the BI rate, will increase in coming months as inflation jumps on a fuel policy adjustment, whether via rationing or a price increase.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  has chosen not to be pre-emptive, despite the dangers of an inflation overshoot from fuel prices."\par \par \uc2 FAUZI ICHSAN, ECONOMIST, STANDARD CHARTERED, JAKARTA\par \par \uc2 "This was to be expected. We do expect them to keep rates on hold for the rest of the year. Though we think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase the FASBI rate by 50 basis points by the second half of this year."\par \par \uc2 GARETH LEATHER, ECONOMIST, CAPITAL ECONOMICS, SINGAPORE\par \par \uc2 "The recent sharp rise in inflation increases the chances of a rate hike before the end of the year. However, given the uncertain global outlook and the fact that core inflation remains subdued, we believe interest rates will remain on hold for the remainder of 2013.\par \par \uc2 "The big question now is how long it will be before BI starts to hike interest rates. The worsening of the current account position, falling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the weak rupiah and continued strong credit growth are all reasons why BI may decide to hike rates sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 "One area on uncertainty concerns the outlook for inflation...so far the pick-up has been driven by temporary factors, which have pushed up food prices. More importantly, core inflation is low, which suggests that underlying price pressures remain relatively subdued. Provided that core inflation remains low, BI is likely to take a relaxed view about the increase in the headline rate.\par \par \uc2 "With exports set to struggle this year on account of weak global demand, we expect interest rates to remain on hold until the end of 2013, with policy tightening likely to begin next year."\par \par \uc2 GUNDY CAHYADI, ECONOMIST, \uc2 OCBC BANK\uc2 , SINGAPORE\par \par \uc2 "BI's statement seemed a little more dovish than usual, and certainly the downward revision to the 2013 GDP growth forecast from 6.6 percent to 6.2 percent was a clear indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to maintain their accomodative policy as long as they can.\par \par \uc2 "Indonesia's export growth has been somewhat disappointing in the first 2 months of the year and it seems like the marked recovery that we have been expecting (to happen in H2) may now be delayed.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the recent uptick in inflation, BI remains confident that inflation pressure will start to moderate again in the future, and thus, they stood by the decision to keep the rate unchanged.\par \par \uc2 "Having said that, we think that the eagerly awaited result regarding fuel subsidy will be a key event to watch out for. We suspect that some form of fuel price hike is imminent and that this may necessarily mean that BI needs to raise its BI policy rate to anchor inflationary expectations going forward."\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION\par \par \uc2 - The rupiah spot's indicative prices edged slightly lower after the decision, to 9,695 per U.S. dollar by 0728 GMT.\par \par \uc2 - Jakarta stocks edged lower after the decision but stayed in positive territory, trading up 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT\par \par \uc2 - Four polled analysts expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten policy on Thursday or afterwards by rising the overnight deposit facility rate, or FASBI, to help ease the current account deficit and support the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  cut its policy rate by 75 basis points in 2011, and by another 25 basis points in February 2012 to help maintain the country's annual economic growth at more than 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 - Headline inflation hit a nearly two-year high at 5.9 percent in March due to import restrictions on several foods, but core inflation softened to 4.21 percent from 4.29 percent the previous month.\par \par \uc2 - In 2012, Indonesia had a sizable current account deficit and its first annual trade deficit. The twin deficits put pressure on the rupiah, which weakened about 6 percent against the U.S. dollar during the year.\par \par \uc2 - There was a trade defict of $330 million in February, as global demand stayed weak amid surging imports.\par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves declined to $104.8 billion at the end of March from $105.2 billion in February, as \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 - Recent data releases show modest optimism about the economy. Retail sales in February were 14 percent higher than a year earlier, while a consumer confidence index was flat at 116.8 due to steady domestic consumption.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Andjarsari Paramaditha and Randy Fabi; Editing by Neil Chatterjee)\par \par \uc2 INDONESIA-ECONOMY/RATE (INSTANT VIEW 3) | ABN | D | E | RBN | IF | M   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130414e94b0002p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
