\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  takes precautions to keep rupiah's decline orderly\b0\par\par\uc2 1,287 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2013\par 21:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Indonesian banks told to stay out of offshore rupiah forward market\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cushion rupiah's decline\par \par \uc2 * Politics lengthens odds of a rise in subsidised fuel prices\par \par \uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 14 (Reuters) - \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is gradually closing trading loopholes used by global investors in an attempt to exert more control over its weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prevent a desirable depreciation of the rupiah from turning into a panicky tailspin.\par \par \uc2 Last week, it reminded domestic banks they are not allowed to dabble in offshore rupiah forward markets. It told them it wants an improvement in onshore trading volume. And it said it will specify the hours during which banks can quote exchange rates for the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 Looking for motives behind this focus on trading hours and rules in what has been a relatively well-behaved market, analysts suspect \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's aim is to remove room for speculative play before letting the market take the rupiah down slowly.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling dollars, limiting the rupiah's decline so far this year to around 0.5 percent, but any intention to manage an orderly decline in the rupiah remains unstated.\par \par \uc2 But, without that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupiah would undoubtedly have fallen faster as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes under growing pressure from the country's huge import bills and the risk of capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 The current account showed a deficit of $24.18 billion in 2012, following a surplus of $1.7 billion in 2011, according to data released on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah lost around 6 percent against the dollar in 2012, cushioned by constant, yet subtle \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Hartadi Sarwono said last month the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would use various instruments to maintain the stability of the rupiah, and must from time to time \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease the pressure on dollar supply created by large, lumpy payments to meet energy imports.\par \par \uc2 But Sarwono stressed that Indonesia's balance of payments remained in overall surplus, as the capital account surplus covered the current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 The current account showed a deficit of $7.76 billion in the fourth quarter of 2012, and the deficit for the full year was $24.18 billion, marking a sharp deterioration from the $1.7 billion surplus posted in 2011, according to data released on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia ended 2012 with a balance of payments surplus of $165 million, down sharply from $11.9 billion the previous year, though it had a surplus of $3.2 billion in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 A textbook policy response to pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be for \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  to raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 That could forestall outflows, crimp domestic consumption, and reduce any rise in payments for dollar-priced imports of refined oil products, machinery and food, particularly after the country suffered its first-ever trade deficit in 2012.\par \par \uc2 But investors and economists alike suspect authorities in Southeast Asia's largest economy are going to take the more politically acceptable route of letting the rupiah weaken, albeit at a pace that keeps foreign investors comfortable while also boosting the value of exports and making imports expensive.\par \par \uc2 "What's going to be most palatable is to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drift lower, if that's the way the market is pushing it, with obviously some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the pace of depreciation," said Robert Prior-Wandesforde, Asian economist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "That is not the most appropriate response, in my view."\par \par \uc2 If a massaged depreciation is the ultimate intent, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a subtle fashion, to avoid unsettling foreign investors or unecessarily draw too heavily on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors hold about $28 billion, or 33 percent of Indonesia's high-yielding government bonds.\par \par \uc2 And whereas \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, at $109 billion, and equivalent to six months of imports, appear to be a comfortable amount the bank will want to spend them wisely.\par \par \uc2 Rupiah markets are not pricing in abrupt swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , quite the contrary. Economists expect there will be a slow drift down in the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 ING economist \uc2 Tim Condon\uc2  said Indonesia's current account will remain in deficit, as it did before the 1998 Asian financial crisis, resulting in a 3-to-4 percent annual depreciation in the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 "Hot money investors get used to orderly rupiah depreciation," he warned, adding that investing in Indonesia's fast-growing economy made more sense than investing in rupiah-denominated bonds or stocks.\par \par \uc2 Investors betting on the economy have been going directly into areas such as mining and transport too. Indonesia received a record 221 trillion rupiah ($22.5 billion) in foreign direct investment in 2012.\par \par \uc2 RUPIAH AT 10,000\par \par \uc2 To be fair to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it has little sway over what would be the most effective policy response to reduce the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , namely hikes in heavily subsided fuel prices.\par \par \uc2 Southeast Asia's largest economy is growing between 6 and 7 percent a year. The rapid pace of investment and consumption in the economy has driven imports up, whereas external demand for its coal and other commodity exports has been subdued.\par \par \uc2 Taken together, the trade deficit and the interest payments to foreigners has left Indonesia vulnerable balancing its external books.\par \par \uc2 "If the current account is not stabilising, they really have to go back to the policy basket and pull out something fresh," said Claudio Piron, a strategist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "That is further policy tightening or allowing more depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But I think ultimately the issue is the oil deficit, which they can do something about."\par \par \uc2 The quick fix to the rupiah's woes would be to hike subsidised fuel prices, which on average are responsible for about a billion dollars of the trade deficit each month.\par \par \uc2 But raising prices would be unpopular with voters, who go to the polls for a presidential election in 2014.\par \par \uc2 With inflation also comfortably in the middle of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 3.5-5.5 percent target range, it is reasonable to suppose \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's preferred policy choice is to allow a gentle weakening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The tricky part, though, would be for \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  to manage the pace of that depreciation in a way that forestalls any panic among foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Two factors could unnerve them: extreme volatility or any sign that the rupiah will move more than it has done historically, which is on average 7 to 12 percent a year.\par \par \uc2 Most immediately, that would mean the rupiah, which is being traded around 9,700 a dollar, does not weaken beyond 10,150 in February.\par \par \uc2 Barring a brief bout of weakness in January, foreign investors have so far kept their faith. Market pricing of future rupiah volatility is low and stable.\par \par \uc2 Even the non-deliverable forward (NDF) market, the playground for speculators, belies any sign of concern, with a 3-month pricing for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  just one percent weaker at 9,800 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 And that raises the risk that \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will inadvertently stir unease by firing missives at an offshore market whose stability has been reassuring for foreigners, who hedge their rupiah bets in NDFs, instead of focusing on fixing the real issues with the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 "The irony is that the Indonesian NDF market has been relatively well behaved," said Piron. "So, it may be more harmful than good," he said, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reminder on the ban on NDF trading last week.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDONESIA/RUPIAH (ANALYSIS) | ABX | ABN | C | GRO | MTL | SOF | D | E | RBN | IF | M | O | OIL  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130213e92d001cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
