\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/Marketbeat: Weakened Indonesian Rupiah Could Mean Bargains for Bond Investors\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ewen Chew   \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2013\par 07:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah has weakened sharply in recent months, edging close to the crucial threshold of 10,000 to the U.S. dollar due to several factors including a widening current account deficit. But some traders say that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't let the rate cross that line in the sand, and that the bottoming-out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could attract foreign bond buyers.\par \par \uc2 Investors are showing keen interest in an Indonesian bond auction scheduled for Tuesday--with the relatively cheap rupiah providing a draw, on top of the high yield of Indonesia's government bonds--says a Jakarta-based rupiah trader who works for a foreign investment bank.\par \par \uc2 The dollar-rupiah touched a high of 9,920 last week in Indonesia's "shadow market," the trader says. Before \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, providing $50 million-$100 million in liquidity, the dollar had been trading around 9,870 rupiah. The unofficial market, which has emerged because \uc2 Bank of Indonesia\uc2  restricts its dollar supply, reflects the rupiah's real value more accurately than the official one.\par \par \uc2 The trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably won't let the dollar-rupiah cross the 10,000 mark, as this could trigger a deluge of rupiah-selling due to automatic orders. He favors the idea of gradually selling dollars on the shadow market.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  has a limited supply of foreign reserves and usually sells dollars only to market participants who can prove they've sold rupiah assets. For others who are "pre-hedging" and may not have actually sold the underlying assets, there is the shadow market, which operates outside the formal exchange and where the dollar trades at a premium to the official spot rate.\par \par \uc2 There is also the nondeliverable forward or NDF market, an offshore platform often used by speculators. The NDF market is settled in U.S. dollars using the difference between the contracted NDF rate and the prevailing spot rate, and usually trades at an even higher premium to the official spot rate.\par \par \uc2 Traders closely watch the premiums for dollar-rupiah NDF contracts as a gauge of fear among investors, as they can be a precursor of more volatility in the spot market. In May 2012, when the one-month NDF premium spiked as high as 200 rupiah, a 3.9% rally in the dollar-rupiah spot rate came days later. Last week's rise in the NDF premiums is creating a reinforcing cycle of rupiah weakness and making some market-watchers think it could depreciate further, the Australia & New Zealand banking group's research team said in a recent note.\par \par \uc2 However, an NDF trader in a small, Singapore-based foreign investment bank thinks otherwise. He feels the NDF premiums could slide soon as \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quell market instability. He is looking to bet on a drop in longer-term NDF premiums, such as the one-year contract, which currently trades with a premium of 410 rupiah. This could happen if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  floods the spot market with more dollars in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 At current spot levels, the rupiah could start looking more attractive to investors, the Jakarta-based rupiah dealer says, noting the relatively cheap \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes Indonesia's high-yielding bonds an even better deal. The 10-year Indonesia government bond yields approximately 5.2%, which stands out sharply against a global background of depressed interest rates brought on by round after round of quantitative easing, and is a stark reminder why Indonesia has in the past been a darling of emerging market investors searching for yield.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@dowjones.com [ 01-15-13 0145ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020130115e91f0000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
