\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won, Taiwan dollar up, but  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  loses in mixed trade\b0\par\par\uc2 1,002 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2012\par 02:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * S.Korean exporters support won; \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caution caps\par \par \uc2 * Taiwan dlr up on inflows of foreign financial institutions\par \par \uc2 * Rupiah down on corp dlr demand, but bond inflows seen\par \par \uc2 * Sing dlr dips on market talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jongwoo Cheon\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 2 (Reuters) - The South Korean won and the Taiwan dollar edged up on Friday, supported by improving U.S. economic data, but other emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped on expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local authorities to stem the rise of the regional units.\par \par \uc2 Investors were also cautious ahead of the U.S. Presidential election on Nov. 6 and the challenge of the "fiscal cliff" that Congress must tackle soon after.\par \par \uc2 A rise in U.S. private sector employment in October and a jump in consumer confidence helped push the won and the Taiwan dollar higher as inflows from foreign financial institutions increased.\par \par \uc2 But the Indonesian rupiah slid as local corporates sought dollars while the Singapore dollar eased on speculation that local authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent it from strengthening past 1.2200 to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Suresh Kumar Ramanathan, head of regional interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at CIMB Investment Bank, said he expected emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lose ground after the U.S. election.\par \par \uc2 "Global risk appetite will skew towards cautious tone for the rest of the world post-November 6 Presidential election," said Ramanathan in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 After the election, Congress must deal with a "fiscal cliff" -- up to $600 billion in expiring tax cuts and spending reductions that are set to kick in next year -- which threatens to hurt the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Investors' immediate focus, however, is the U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due at 1230 GMT, which is expected to show employers added 125,000 jobs in October, with the jobless rate ticking up to 7.9 percent from September's 7.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Any upside surprise on this front, we think, may fuel global risk-taking behavior instead of boosting the dollar across the board," \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 On the week, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been mixed after most of them enjoyed gains in the previous week.\par \par \uc2 The won rose 0.6 percent against the dollar and the Taiwan dollar gained 0.3 percent, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit has fallen 0.5 percent with dollar-short covering, while the rupiah has eased 0.3 percent, data for the week showed.\par \par \uc2 WON\par \par \uc2 The won gained on exporters' demand for settlements in extremely thin trading, while its upside was tightly limited as investors stayed cautious over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities.\par \par \uc2 The authorities were spotted overnight trying to stem the won's strength via the non-deliverable forwards market, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Still, investors expect the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stay firm despite uncertainty over the U.S. election next week, dealers added.\par \par \uc2 "There are few reasons to push down the won, given continuous exporters' deals and firm stocks. The won will be around the current levels," said a foreign bank dealer in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar gained as foreign financial institutions bought the local unit on expectations for strong U.S. jobs data later in the day.\par \par \uc2 Dealers in Taiwan expected the island's unit to appreciate more regardless of the U.S. election result, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upside is seen as limited because of the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 The rupiah eased on dollar bids from local corporates, but bond inflows relieved the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.\par \par \uc2 But some dealers expect demand for the bonds not to last long, saying the rupiah is likely to stay under pressure.\par \par \uc2 "These inflows are surprising. But I expect if there are any more inflows, they will be linked to window dressing before the year-end," said a Jakarta based dealer.\par \par \uc2 Another trader pointed out risks from proposed hikes in electricity tariff next year and a prospective fuel price raise.\par \par \uc2 On Oct. 23, Indonesia's parliament approved the government's 2013 budget plan and left open the possibility of a fuel price hike. ID:nL3E8LN3TG]\par \par \uc2 Indonesia does plan to cut power subsidies next year by hiking electricity tariffs by 15 percent, to save 11.8 trillion rupiah ($1.23 billion) that will be used to improve infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 The trader said the rupiah could weaken to 9,700 per dollar by the end of the year, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will slow down, especially in December.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Change on the day at 0615 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     