\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE: \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  Leaves Rate Unchanged Amid Weakening Growth, Rupiah Pressure\b0\par\par\uc2 1,023 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2012\par 13:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  holds rates steady, governor says inflation under control\par \par \uc2 --Focus on rupiah, inflationary pressures signal bias toward tightening policy\par \par \uc2 --Weaker-than-expected growth predicted in 2012 and 2013\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  may allow onshore banks to swap U.S. dollar bonds for dollars\par \par \uc2 --Current-account deficit likely widened in second quarter\par \par \uc2 (Adds context throughout, current-account deficit predictions in 13th paragraph, news of dollar-bond swaps in 14th and 15th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 By Ben Otto, I Made Sentana, Joko Hariyanto\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA--Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its policy interest rate unchanged at 5.75% as expected Thursday, as higher inflation and continued weakness in the rupiah kept it from relaxing its stance despite weaker growth forecasts associated with the global economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia Gov. Darmin Nasution reiterated the bank's commitment to keeping inflation under control and focus on the rupiah. He told reporters that "the current policy rate is consistent with the inflation forecast" and reiterated past comments that inflation "is likely to remain low and can be contained within our target range" of 3.5%-5.5%.\par \par \uc2 The decision to hold rates at an all-time low comes as Indonesia is facing weakening growth while trying to keep a lid on inflation, and as countries around the region are loosening policy. Bank of Korea unexpectedly cut its benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points Thursday, and China has cut its key rate twice in the past month, but economists say a weak rupiah will keep \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  from following suit for the rest of the year.\par \par \uc2 "As before, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  indicated that it will keep the rupiah in line with the rest of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggesting that further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out flows in the market will remain prevalent going forward," OCBC economist Gundy Cahyadi said in an email. "We expect inflation to average around 5.0%-5.5% this year, and that \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will not adjust its monetary policy rate anytime soon."\par \par \uc2 All 13 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires had predicted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would maintain its overnight rate after inflation accelerated to 4.53% in June from 4.45% in May, stoked by higher prices of basic foodstuffs.\par \par \uc2 The country's financial markets had little reaction to the news. The rupiah was steady at IDR9,460 against the dollar immediately after the decision, later weakening to IDR9,480 on regional concern and importers' demand for dollars. The stock market closed down 0.9%, a slight recovery from deeper losses earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said Indonesian commercial banks increased their loans by 26.3% during the first five months of the year, a sign of continued improvement in their intermediary role. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been encouraging banks to lend to boost economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cahyadi said strong loan growth amid still robust domestic demand could push inflation to 6.3%-6.5% next year.\par \par \uc2 Inflation will likely stay elevated in July and August due to the start of a new school year and the high spending associated with the Islamic fasting month of Ramadan.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Nasution also said the bank had cut growth forecasts for 2012 and 2013. The bank now sees 2012 growth of 6.1%-6.5%, whereas last month it said growth would likely be at the bottom end of its 6.3%-6.7% forecast range due to debt crisis in the euro zone, a fragile U.S. recovery and slowdowns in China and India. He added that the bank projects third-quarter growth of 6.3%.\par \par \uc2 In 2013 the bank now also predicts expansion 6.3%-6.7%, down from 6.4%-6.8% previously.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah has clawed back some of its losses recently but remains a regional laggard due to expectations that Indonesia's current account will remain in deficit in the third and fourth quarters. Gov. Nasution said Thursday the current account deficit likely widened in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 The bank has said it predicts a current-account deficit of about $3 billion in 2012, but some analysts have said it could be almost three times that amount. The deficit will add more pressure on Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which have decreased by some $10 billion in the last two months, to about $106.5 billion at the end of June, as \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to stave off rupiah volatility.\par \par \uc2 In an unexpected move, Deputy Governor \uc2 Halim Alamsyah\uc2  also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may allow onshore banks to swap U.S. dollar bonds for dollars.\par \par \uc2 The arrangement would be "marginally positive in terms of providing US dollar liquidity," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said in a research note. "That said, the immediate impact might be limited as banks that have U.S. dollar bonds are more likely to have cash dollars as well, and hence would be unlikely to tap this window."\par \par \uc2 Dollar liquidity has been a problem in Indonesia, where risk-averse foreigners recently pushed the rupiah the lows not seen in almost four years as they sought dollars to exit the market. \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  introduced dollar term deposits in June in a bid to attract more offshore dollars into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Still, demand for dollars remains high and the rupiah remains Southeast Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, having fallen about 4% year to date and more than 9% in the last 12 months.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Nasution reiterated the bank's stance that the U.S. dollar would average IDR9,200 this year.\par \par \uc2 "Prospects for the rupiah will remain poor, given widening trade and current account deficits," Hak Bin Chua of \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  said in a research note. "Given weak exports, the current account deficit could widen in excess of $5 billion, or about 2.4% of GDP, in the second quarter. Falling foreign reserves could also restrain \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to stabilize the rupiah."\par \par \uc2 Write to Ben Otto at ben.otto@dowjones.com, I Made Sentana at i-made.sentana@dowjones.com and Joko Hariyanto at jokems@gmail.com [ 12-07-12 1149GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120712e87c000v7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
