\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Indonesia Deputy Gov: To Continue Supplying Market With Dollars\b0\par\par\uc2 By Farida Husna   \par \uc2 177 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2012\par 06:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA--Bank Indonesia Deputy Governor Hartadi Sarwono again said Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue pumping dollars into the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the rupiah from falling sharply.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia also expected exporters and others to also start selling their dollars, Mr. Sarwono said.\par \par \uc2 The comment came after he told Dow Jones Newswires Tuesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to supply the market with U.S. dollars to prevent the rupiah from falling excessively.\par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have increased its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since last week, as dealers said the scarcity of dollars in the market was partly responsible for the rupiah's fall late last month to its lowest levels since December 2009.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said exporters and foreign investors appear to have started selling their dollars Thursday after the dollar fell sharply against the euro amid hopes of resolution on the euro-zone crisis.\par \par \uc2 -Write to Farida Husna at farida.husna@dowjones.com [ 07-06-12 0435GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120607e8670005v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
