\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Ringgit touches 8-month low, growth worries weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2012\par 06:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Ringgit touches 8-month low; Taiwan dollar hits 4-month low\par \par \uc2 * Singapore dollar shows some resilience\par \par \uc2 * Dollar faces resistance vs Singapore dollar near 1.30\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comments)\par \par \uc2 By Masayuki Kitano\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 4 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit hit an eight-month low and the Taiwan dollar touched a four-month trough on Monday on heightened worries about global growth after data last week showed surprisingly weak U.S. job growth in May.\par \par \uc2 Most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were weaker, although the Singapore dollar showed some resilience and pulled away from a five-month low set last week.\par \par \uc2 Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have slid over the past month on fears that Greece may exit the euro zone and worries that Spain may need external aid to shore up its ailing banking sector, and worries of a global economic slowdown have added to their woes.\par \par \uc2 The weak U.S. jobs data and the chaos in Europe have put "fear into the market", said a trader for a Malaysian bank in Kuala Lumpur. "Risk aversion is the main theme," he said, adding that some market players were looking to cover their short dollar positions.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to as high as 3.2050 versus the ringgit at one point, its highest since early October, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par \par \uc2 Dollar buying by hedge funds and interbank traders helped drag the ringgit lower, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Against the Taiwan dollar, the greenback climbed to its highest level since mid-January of 30.055.\par \par \uc2 The drop in the ringgit and Taiwan dollar came after data on Friday showed that U.S. employers added a paltry 69,000 jobs to their payrolls last month, the least since May of last year.\par \par \uc2 The weak reading has "very negative" implications for emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Dariusz Kowalczyk, senior economist and strategist for \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "Weakening of the U.S. consumer means less demand for Asian exports, which will weigh on external positions and growth," he said, adding that risks for emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were tilted toward the downside in the near term, although the market may have already priced in a lot of bad news.\par \par \uc2 A recent Reuters survey underscored how market sentiment toward emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently shifted. The poll on market positioning published on May 24 showed that market players turned bearish on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Malaysian ringgit and the Taiwan dollar, and also increased their short positions in the South Korean won to the largest in more than three years.\par \par \uc2 The dollar may now be due for a pullback against emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said a trader for a major Japanese bank in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "Personally, I feel the dollar/Asians (are) looking like a sell now," the trader said, adding that the market may have reached a point where the dollar has become overbought against emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The dollar dipped 0.2 percent versus the Singapore dollar to 1.2906, retreating from Friday's peak of 1.2972, its highest level against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since early January.\par \par \uc2 The dollar faces resistance near 1.3000, which roughly coincides with the 76.4 percent retracement of the greenback's slide from October 2011 to April 2012.\par \par \uc2 INDONESIAN RUPIAH\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah, the second-worst performing emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year behind the Indian rupee according to Thomson Reuters data of indicative prices, gained some support due to dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and traders were wary about the possibility of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 Change on the day at 0645 GMT\par \par \uc2 *Thailand's stock, bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are closed on Monday for a national \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par holiday.       \tab \par                                                                        \par  Japan yen        78.18          78.07       -0.14\par  Sing dlr        1.2906         1.2931       +0.19\par  Taiwan dlr      30.043         29.931       -0.37\par  Korean won     1182.27        1177.70       -0.39\par  Baht             31.80          31.89       +0.28\par  Peso             43.47          43.40       -0.16\par  Rupiah         9475.00        9380.00       -1.00\par  Rupee            55.55          55.54       -0.02\par  Ringgit         3.1995         3.1930       -0.20\par  Yuan            6.3679         6.3690       +0.02\par                                                                        \par  Change so far in 2012                                                   \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        78.18          76.92       -1.61\par  Sing dlr        1.2906         1.2969       +0.49\par  Taiwan dlr      30.043        