\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Sharp Slowdown In Asia Manufacturing Sounds Ominous Warning\b0\par\par\uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2012\par 05:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- China PMIs show slower manufacturing activity in May\par \par \uc2 -- Korea, Taiwan and Australia manufacturing slows or contracts\par \par \uc2 -- Exports fall in Korea, Indonesia; inflation benign\par \par \uc2 -- Data show the impact of crises in the West \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Michael S. Arnold \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones) Manufacturing activity in China and across a wide swath of Asia slowed in May, heightening fears that the turmoil in Western economies is dragging down one of the few remaining engines of global growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two purchasing managers indexes for China fell in May. The official one, based on government data, showed manufacturing continuing to grow but by the barest of margins, falling to 50.4 in May from 53.3 in April. A figure above 50 indicates expansion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A private index produced by \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Markit showed Chinese manufacturing contracting ever more quickly, falling to 48.4 in May from 49.3 a month earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As risks of a hard landing have increased, Beijing should issue more policies to stabilize economic growth," said Li Huiyong, an economist at Shenyin & Wanguo Securities. "The quicker interest rates are cut, the better."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data come as Europe's spiraling debt crisis and the sluggish U.S. recovery are washing up on Asian shores in the form of weaker demand for exports and capital flight from risk. Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Indian rupee and Indonesian rupiah have plunged in recent weeks, forcing governments to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  almost daily to support them and take steps to encourage investors to stay put.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stock markets, which began the day weaker on global concerns about Europe, slipped further on the weak data, led by Taiwan, down 1.5% around 0330 GMT. Trading was cautious as the weekend approached and with European PMIs and U.S. nonfarm payroll data still on tap. Shanghai shares, though, were 0.5% higher on expectations that the sharp slowdown will prompt Beijing to take aggressive measures to shore up growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also extended their declines, with traders saying Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the rupiah.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The stark exception in Asia, the yen, has served as a safe haven for risk-averse investors, boosting the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to three-month highs against the dollar and undermining the key export sector. Japanese government officials ratcheted up their rhetoric Friday, vowing "decisive" action against "excessive" yen moves -- thinly veiled threats to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen for the first time since early November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's dour manufacturing readings were mirrored across Asia on Friday. A PMI for Australia fell deep into contractionary territory, down 1.5 points in May to 42.4. Indexes in South Korea and Taiwan remained barely positive but both showed growth slowing, with South Korea's \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  PMI falling to 51.0 from 51.9, and Taiwan's to 50.5 from 51.2.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Slower output growth shows that Korean manufacturers remain cautious, partly reflecting the recent escalation of external uncertainties," \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Economist Ronald Man said in a report on the data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Korea's exports, considered a bellwether for the region, fell 0.4% in May from a year earlier for a third straight decline, a shock for an economy that depends on them and a surprise for the market, which had expected a 0.7% gain. Exports to Europe and the U.S. plunged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia, where a large domestic market provides some protection from global ructions, also saw its exports plunge. They fell 3.46% on-year in April and 7.36% on-month for a trade deficit of $640 million, the first in nearly two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One bright spot was inflation, which has remained benign as economic growth slows. Inflation in Indonesia eased slightly to 4.45% on year in May from April's 4.5%. Consumer prices in South Korea rose 2.5%, well within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's comfort zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Michael S. Arnold, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4043; michael.arnold@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 31, 2012 23:38 ET (03:38 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120601e861000v7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
