\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Bank Indonesia Confident 'Large'  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserves Can Defend Rupiah\b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2012\par 07:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Bank Indonesia can draw on new dollar deposits to defend the rupiah\par \par \uc2 -- BI projects the dollar to average IDR9,100 to IDR9,300 in 2012 and 2013\par \par \uc2 -- BI expects capital inflows to rise in the second half\par \par \uc2 (Adds background on Indonesia's current account deficit in the sixth paragraph; latest dollar/rupiah rate in the eighth paragraph; and comments from the BI governor in the ninth through 13th paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Farida Husna \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia Gov. Darmin Nasution Thursday said he is confident the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be able to defend the rupiah as it is armed with "large" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Its reserves totaled $113.1 billion as of Tuesday, equal to six months of imports and debt repayment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia Tuesday said it would offer U.S. dollar term deposits within the next two weeks, to encourage banks to keep their dollars at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  instead of offshore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nasution Thursday told reporters Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can use the dollars in the term deposits to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if necessary, by selling them in the spot or swap markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fear of Greece exiting the euro zone along with concern over Indonesia's widening current account deficit have sent the rupiah to its lowest level against the dollar since December 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia booked a $2.9 billion current account deficit in the January-March quarter, widening from a $1.6 billion deficit in October-December due to robust imports of capital goods and petroleum products.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers estimate the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to have sold close to $1 billion so far this week to defend the rupiah.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was at IDR9,550 at 0520 GMT, higher than IDR9,425 late Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But during a parliamentary hearing Thursday Nasution said Bank Indonesia will be able to push the dollar lower to around IDR9,450 later in the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said the rupiah will likely average IDR9,100 to IDR9,300 this year and next. The government assumed a 2012 average of IDR9,000 when it compiled its budget.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nasution also said capital inflows will likely increase in the second half of the year because the global economic situation will likely improve.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "From a fundamental perspective, global economic improvement in the second half of the year will improve the performance of [our] exports and current account, which will attract more capital inflows than in the first half of the year," Nasution said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia continues to expect inflation to be within its target range of 3.5% and 5.5% this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Farida Husna, Dow Jones Newswires; +62 21-39831277; farida.husna@dowjones.com [ 31-05-12 0530GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120531e85v0009t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
