\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-FULL TEXT-Indonesia c.bank holds rates steady\b0\par\par\uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2012\par 10:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, April 12 (Reuters) - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its key interest rate unchanged at a record low of 5.75 percent on Thursday, as expected, as March inflation remained low and after parliament derailed a government plan to raise fuel prices this month.\par \par \uc2  For more news on the rate decision, see \par \par \uc2  Following is the full text of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement:\par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia will continue strengthening monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and macroprudential policies by consistently keeping interest rates with macroeconomic development going forward. By coordinating with the government through inflation management teams in the central and regional levels, Bank Indonesia is confident to bring 2013 inflation towards 4.5 plus minus 1 percent.\par \par \uc2  The board of governors views that the global economy is still clouded by high uncertainty. While the U.S. economy has shown improvement, recovery in Europe is still hampered by the ongoing crisis resolution and there are indications of slowdown in China and India. A global economic slowdown is estimated to further reduce exports of emerging markets including Indonesia.\par \par \uc2  Global inflationary pressures are still relatively low so developed economies still continue accommodative policies despite with limited room. But rising global commodity prices, particularly oil, have increased inflationary pressures that can push tighter monetary policy in emerging markets. Volatility of foreign capital inflows to emerging markets is estimated to continue.\par \par \uc2  The board of governors estimate Indonesia's economic growth will still be relatively high amid risks of global economic slowdown and possible government policy on fuel prices. In the second quarter growth is estimated reaching 6.4%, slightly lower than the first quarter at 6.5%. Growth is estimated to reach 6.3-6.7% in 2012 and rise to 6.4-6.8% in 2013. Balance of risks for 2012 shows that growth tends to bias downwards because of global economic slowdown and possible fuel price policy by the government, if without stimulus particularly from the fiscal side.\par \par \uc2  Indonesia's balance of payments in 2012 is estimated to record a lower surplus than the previous year. That is mainly due to a bigger current account deficit because of slowing exports. Capital and financial transaction is estimated to still record a quite high surplus supported by foreign direct and portfolio investments. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves until end-March were still quite big at $110.5 billion, equal with 6.1 months of imports and foreign debt payments.\par \par \uc2  The rupiah in the first quarter weakened. Point-to-point it weakened 0.83% to 9,144 per U.S. dollar and in average 1.03% quarter-to-quarter to 9,066. The weakening was followed by rising volatility due compared to the previous quarter. Pressures to the rupiah were from foreign portfolio adjustments due to global sentiments and rising domestic inflation expectations, aside from rising \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand for imports. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rupiah stabilisation by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or buying government bonds in the secondary market made the rupiah movement still maintained.\par \par \uc2  Inflation was still manageable although there are risks of rising inflationary pressures going forward. CPI inflation in the first quarter was at 0.88% quarter-to-quarter and 3.97% year-on-year, higher than the previous quarter. Going forward, Bank Indonesia will remain vigilant on the risks of rising inflation expectations particularly due to fuel price policy that could be pursued by the government.\par \par \uc2  Banking system stability remained stable and followed by better intermediary functions in supporting economic financing. Loan growth as of end-February reached 24.2% year-on-year. Investment loans grew quite high at 33.2% and are expected to raise economic capacity. While capital working loans and consumer loans each grew by 23.4% and 19.6%.\par \par \uc2  Going forward, the board of governors will learn the impact of possible government policy related to fuel prices and the risks of global economic slowdown to Indonesia. If the fuel price policy is pursued by the government, Bank Indonesia remains confident the impact to inflation will be temporary (one-time shock). However, Bank Indonesia will optimise various policies needed to minimise the temporary impact and manage inflationary pressures based on fundamentals to be as targeted.\par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia will strengthen the mixture of monetary and macroprudential policies that has been pursued. Interest rate responses are still aimed at managing inflationary pressures based on fundamentals according to future macroeconomic forecasts. Meanwhile to manage short-term and temporary inflationary pressures, the policies will be focused on strengthening monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and managing excess liquidity. Coordination with the government will be intensified.     \par \par \uc2 (Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 INDONESIA-ECONOMY/CBANK-STATEMENT-TEXT (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | IF | M | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120412e84c000k4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
