\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Higher;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Expected - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,510 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2012\par 02:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is higher at 9,200 vs 9,180 late Tuesday as fresh inflows remain scanty and Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yet, dealers say. "But I expect the dollar may slide to 9,180 again in the afternoon because I expect Bank Indonesia will sell dollars, perhaps around $100 million in total," a dealer says. He tips a 9,180-9,220 range. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is rangebound, with importers' buying providing solid support while some exporters are still willing to sell on rises, say two local traders. The pair is at 29.981 vs 29.995 late Tuesday; it was mostly traded at 29.950 Tuesday before suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  near the close, traders say. "If local stocks are able to rise over 1.0%, the greenback is likely to test 29.900 later," one of the traders says. But the second trader says that based on recent evidence the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still likely to lift the pair above 30.000 in the afternoon. They tip the pair to trade in a 29.900-30.020 band for the session. The Taiex is up 0.3%. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 0.1% at 19,654.73, taking a breather after rallying 3.2% Tuesday, but the market's near-term bias is likely to remain on the upside. Market volume is solid at HK$8.27 billion. \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2  says that technically, the HSI broke through December's high, and the MACDs gaps are expanding, "showing that momentum turning positive." It tips the HSI's first resistance would be 20,000, then it may eye October's high of 20,272. Blue chips are about half up and half down, with Tuesday's outperformers succumb to light profit-taking: Chalco (2600.HK) is down 0.8% at HK$3.83 while Ping An (2318.HK) edges down 0.1% to HK$55.30. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open higher, on a China Securities Journal report that the government is mulling tax breaks for institutional investors in a bid to encourage long-term investment. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.5% at 2309.46, and is expected to consolidate around 2300 for most of the session. Yang Delong, a fund manager at China Southern Fund, says the market looks attractive for institutional investors like pension funds after declines in the past two years; still, "the market's performance will depend on how significant the macro easing is as a result of deceleration in growth." Metal and mining companies extend gains as China's 4Q economic data eased concerns over a hard landing in the economy; China Nonferrous Metal (000758.SZ) gains 3.9% to CNY20.45, Yunnan Copper (000878.SZ) rises 2.9% to CNY18.40, Chalco (601600.SH) is up 3.3% at CNY7.56. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.9% at 867.89. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR will likely remain under pressure as Greece resumes talks on debt restructuring plans and Portugal tests investor confidence with a debt sale later in the day, says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Tokyo. The EUR/USD will likely stay near 1.2750 during Asian trading hours Wednesday amid a lack of fresh leads, he says, tipping the pair in a 1.2550-1.2850 band over the next week. Greece's default risk presents a "negative risk" for the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as reports suggest the debt-ridden nation may default in the coming months, he says. "I am not optimistic on the situation," he says. The EUR/USD is at 1.2760.(alexander.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold and silver demand in India is expected to slow after India's government increases import taxes, says Mitsui Global Precious Metals in a note. Earlier this week, the Indian government said gold imports will be taxed at 2% of value vs a flat rate of INR300 ($5.90) per 10 grams and silver imports will be taxed at 6% of value from a flat rate of INR1,500/kg. "With this in mind, reports today that India has increased taxes on purchasing gold and silver should have only a minor short-term impact on the market as a whole, although we expect domestic demand to be slow for a while as buyers adjust to the new rates," says Mitsui. India is historically the biggest consumer. Spot gold is at $1,647.30/oz, down $4.30, and silver is at $29.95/oz, down 11 cents from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is little changed at 31.77-31.79 from 31.79 late Tuesday in Asia on improving risk sentiment following positive economic data out of the U.S. and Germany Tuesday as well as the successful sale of EUR4.88 billion worth of T-bills in Spain. However, the downside is likely to be limited due to ongoing concerns over euro-zone debt woes. The USD/THB is expected to move in a tight range Wednesday "as importers are waiting to buy dollars at a lower levels, while exporters prefer to sell dollars at a higher rate," says a dealer at a local bank. She tips resistance for the pair at 31.85, with support at 31.75. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0118 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD edges down to 7.7651 from 7.7662 late Tuesday, tracking the greenback's weakness abroad although the EUR pares its gains overnight that were driven by a well-received auctions for short-term bills in Europe, says a trader at a Singapore bank. "Still, the Hong Kong stock market is likely biased up, given strong A-share momentum. This will continuously weigh on the pair," he says. He tips the pair will have solid support at 7.7620, while resistance is pegged at 7.7680 today. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are likely to extend Tuesday's 1.9% rise as positive economic data in Germany and the U.S. Tuesday coupled with China's stronger-than-expected 4Q GDP growth are expected to lift market sentiment. Better-than-expected 2011 earnings from TMB Bank (TMB.TH) may prompt a buying spree in banking stocks, says KT Zmico Securities First Vice President Charoen Iamphattanatham. TMB Bank Tuesday reported 2011 net profit of THB4.01 billion, up from THB3.20 billion a year earlier; Thailand's four largest commercial banks are scheduled to announce their earnings from Wednesday to Friday. However, uncertainties about euro-zone's debt problems are likely cap the upside. "There's no any negative leads to hit the Thai stock market Wednesday," he adds. Resistance is tipped at 1070, with support at 1050, while the benchmark SET index closed at 1056.54 Tuesday. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may continue to advance following Tuesday's gains in U.S. equities on the back of encouraging economic data out of the U.S., Germany and China. The benchmark PSEi may head towards 4665 resistance after advancing 1.4% Tuesday to 4642.76. "Technically, the market remains slightly above overbought territory. However, there has been no market moving news with a negative spin to deflate optimism that has sent prices higher," says Jun Calaycay, a trader with Accord Capital Equities. He tips next support at 4590. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] Weakening consumer sentiment in Australia could be one facilitator of more rate cuts from the RBA, says \uc2 UBS\uc2  Senior Economist George Tharenou. For January, \uc2 Westpac\uc2 's index of consumer sentiment in Australia rose 2.4% from December, though the overall level still sat below where it was just a few months ago before two recent rate cuts from the RBA. "The lack of a significant improvement in confidence suggests the rate cuts to date are yet to fully gain traction," says Tharenou. The AUD/USD is little changed after the consumer sentiment data and the subsequent report on merchandise imports; it is now at 1.0383.(geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD is up on a local report that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lowered reserve requirements for some local banks, which has spurred buying in riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the AUD and the EUR, traders in Tokyo say. Hedge funds are seen as the main buyers. The pair is at 1.2758 against 1.2737 in late New York trade Tuesday.(tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 17, 2012 20:58 ET (01:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120118e81i000s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
