\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,005 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2011\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par G-20 OFFICIAL: UNCLEAR IF FINANCE MINISTERS' MEETING WILL HAPPEN IN DEC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lack of a solution to the European debt crisis has put into question whether Group of 20 finance ministers will meet before the year-end to discuss bolstering the International Monetary Fund's resources, a G-20 official said Wednesday. (published 0311 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: FRENCH FIN MIN: PRESIDENT SARKOZY WILL UNVEIL EU TREATY CHANGES PROPOSALS DEC 1 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French President Nicolas Sarkozy will unveil France's proposals for changes on the European Union treaty Dec. 1, during a speech in Toulon on the state of the economy, French Finance Minister Francois Baroin said Tuesday. (published 0031 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: EFSF'S REGLING: LEVERAGING UP BAILOUT FUND WILL TAKE TIME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It will take time to increase the leverage of the euro zone's bailout fund and European Union officials are still trying to persuade others to increase the International Monetary Fund's resources in order to help boost the bailout fund, senior EU officials said. (published 0038 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB'S LIIKANEN: EUROPEAN CRISIS COULD TRIGGER NEGATIVE SPIRAL FOR BANKS -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unless the European sovereign-debt crisis gets contained it could trigger a negative spiral for banks, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governing Council member Erkki Liikanen said in an interview with Finnish business daily Kauppalehti published Wednesday. (published 0400 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: ECB'S NOYER: POLICY MAKERS NEED TO STABILIZE EUROPEAN BOND MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Christian Noyer said Wednesday policy makers need to stabilize the European bond market but confidence in the euro remains strong, and that central bankers should remain focused on the task of ensuring price stability. (published 0438 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CONFIDENTIAL REPORT WARNS OF DIRE ITALY CRISIS - GUARDIAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European finance ministers were warned on Tuesday night that Italy's liquidity crisis could leave the euro zone's third biggest economy insolvent, with devastating impact on the fate of the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and its big core economies, Germany and France, U.K. newspaper The Guardian reported on its website Wednesday. (published 0216 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJE(11/30) A SAFER SHELTER? KEY U.K. YIELD BELOW GERMANY'S \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. government's borrowing costs for 10 years fell below those of Germany for the first time since 2009 on Tuesday, as the deepening euro-zone debt crisis spurred investors to trim exposure to even haven German debt and seek refuge elsewhere. (published 0531 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par R&I PLACES JAPAN SOVEREIGN RATING ON REVIEW FOR DOWNGRADE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese rating firm Rating and Investment Information said Wednesday it has placed Japan's triple-A sovereign rating on review for downgrade, citing uncertainty over when the country will reduce its debts to an acceptable level. (published 0635 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT UP 2.4% IN OCT DESPITE THAI FLOODS, EURO CONCERNS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese industrial production rose a stronger than expected 2.4% in October from the previous month, marking the first increase in two months despite the European debt crisis and the effects of the flooding in Thailand. (published 0035 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: BOJ NISHIMURA SAYS EUROPE CRISIS BIGGEST RISK FOR JAPAN OUTLOOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Bank of Japan deputy governor said Wednesday the ongoing euro-zone debt crisis is the biggest risk factor for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's baseline scenario that Japan will eventually return to sustainable growth, warning that there is no quick fix to Europe's problems. (published 0600 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: SOUTH KOREA INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT DATA SHOW SIGNS OF SLOWING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's industrial output grew at a faster-than-expected rate in October, but underlying data pointed to slowing momentum for the export-dependent economy as the euro-zone fiscal crisis pushes the global economy closer to a full-blown slump. (published 0142 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVT: NOV 1-23 EXPORTS UP 15.1% FROM A YEAR EARLIER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's exports in the first 23 days of November were up 15.1% from a year earlier, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Wednesday. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: INDIA GROWS 6.9% IN JULY-SEPT, SLOWEST IN OVER 2 YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian economy expanded at its slowest pace in more than two years in the July-September quarter, hurt by high local borrowing costs and a deepening euro-zone crisis, sparking growth concerns that could lead to an early change in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's tight monetary stance. (published 0653 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA FINANCE MINISTER: ECONOMY LIKELY TO GROW 7.3% THIS FISCAL YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's government expects the economy to grow 7.3% this fiscal year to March 31, 2012, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said Wednesday. (published 0650 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: THAI OCTOBER EXPORTS DOWN 0.1% ON YEAR; IMPORTS UP 20.6% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following are economic indicators for October released by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wednesday. (published 0644 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INDONESIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  GOV: TO BOOST \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will increase \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to prevent the rupiah from falling sharply, BI Gov. Darmin Nasution said Wednesday. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: AUSTRALIAN BUSINESS INVESTMENT SURGES IN 3Q, TO DRIVE STRONG GDP GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian business investment rocketed higher in the third quarter, with a once-in-a-century mining boom pushing economists to raise their growth forecasts for the country. (published 0457 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par HEARD ON THE STREET: SURPLUS PLEDGE HAMPERS AUSTRALIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's budget surplus target is commendable and rare among developed nations. Achieving it, though, will be tough and could hurt parts of the economy already struggling despite the country's mining boom. (published 0650 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA NOVEMBER CPI LIKELY UP 4.20% ON YEAR, 0.33% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's on-year inflation likely slowed a bit in November from October because of a high base of comparison last year, but compared with a month earlier prices rose rather than falling, presenting the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a dilemma as it seeks to ease monetary policy in light of a slowing global economy. (published 0420 GMT) [ 30-11-11 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111130e7bu000cl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
