NEED TO STABILIZE EUROPEAN BOND MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governing council member Christian Noyer said Wednesday policy makers need to stabilize the European bond market but confidence in the euro remains strong, and that central bankers should remain focused on the task of ensuring price stability. (published 0438 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CONFIDENTIAL REPORT WARNS OF DIRE ITALY CRISIS - GUARDIAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European finance ministers were warned on Tuesday night that Italy's liquidity crisis could leave the euro zone's third biggest economy insolvent, with devastating impact on the fate of the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and its big core economies, Germany and France, U.K. newspaper The Guardian reported on its website Wednesday. (published 0216 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJE(11/30) A SAFER SHELTER? KEY U.K. YIELD BELOW GERMANY'S \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. government's borrowing costs for 10 years fell below those of Germany for the first time since 2009 on Tuesday, as the deepening euro-zone debt crisis spurred investors to trim exposure to even haven German debt and seek refuge elsewhere. (published 0531 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT UP 2.4% IN OCT DESPITE THAI FLOODS, EURO CONCERNS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese industrial production rose a stronger than expected 2.4% in October from the previous month, marking the first increase in two months despite the European debt crisis and the effects of the flooding in Thailand. (published 0035 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ NISHIMURA SAYS EUROPEAN DEBT CRISIS BIGGEST RISK FOR JAPAN OUTLOOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Bank of Japan deputy governor said Wednesday the ongoing euro-zone debt crisis is the biggest risk factor for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's baseline scenario that Japan will eventually return to sustainable growth, warning that there is no quick fix to Europe's problems. (published 0347 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: SOUTH KOREA INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT DATA SHOW SIGNS OF SLOWING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's industrial output grew at a faster-than-expected rate in October, but underlying data pointed to slowing momentum for the export-dependent economy as the euro-zone fiscal crisis pushes the global economy closer to a full-blown slump. (published 0142 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVT: NOV 1-23 EXPORTS UP 15.1% FROM A YEAR EARLIER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's exports in the first 23 days of November were up 15.1% from a year earlier, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Wednesday. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: INDIA JULY-SEPT GDP GROWS 6.9% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian economy expanded 6.9% in the July-September quarter from a year earlier, hurt by slower growth in manufacturing output and a contraction in mining production, according to data issued by the Central Statistical Organisation Wednesday. (published 0540 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INDONESIA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  GOV: TO BOOST \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will increase \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to prevent the rupiah from falling sharply, BI Gov. Darmin Nasution said Wednesday. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: AUSTRALIAN BUSINESS INVESTMENT SURGES IN 3Q, TO DRIVE STRONG GDP GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian business investment rocketed higher in the third quarter, with a once-in-a-century mining boom pushing economists to raise their growth forecasts for the country. (published 0457 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA NOVEMBER CPI LIKELY UP 4.20% ON YEAR, 0.33% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's on-year inflation likely slowed a bit in November from October because of a high base of comparison last year, but compared with a month earlier prices rose rather than falling, presenting the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a dilemma as it seeks to ease monetary policy in light of a slowing global economy. (published 0420 GMT) [ 30-11-11 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111130e7bu000af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
