\par\par \b \uc2 DJ FOCUS: Market Turmoil Shakes Image Of Indonesia As New Safe Haven\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2011\par 09:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Recent market turmoil has shaken faith in Indonesia as a safe haven\par \par \uc2 --Indonesia suffering from general hesitation to invest in emerging markets during current turmoil\par \par \uc2 --Authorities have tools, funds available to boost market \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By I Made Sentana \par    OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Capital outflows during August and September's market turmoil shaved 21% from Indonesia's main stock index and drove the rupiah to its weakest point in 15 months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the deeper damage could be more difficult to quantify: The sudden upheaval has shaken the notion that this emerging-market powerhouse is a new safe haven in turbulent times.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Indonesia's relatively thin trading liquidity implies the country's bond and equity markets are exposed to large market volatility, implying the thesis of Indonesia as a 'safe haven' play may not be completely deep-rooted," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 's analyst Teddy Oetomo wrote in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As recently as July, officials crowed as investors infatuated with Indonesia's growth story and improving fiscal position drove the JSX Index to a record high of 4,193 points (vs 3,701 currently), while foreign buying drove yields on rupiah-denominated government bonds to historical lows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Global investors are now avoiding (the U.S. and Europe) and instead they're going to Indonesia, one of the emerging countries with good fundamentals," Director General of State Debt Management Rahmat Waluyanto told Dow Jones Newswires in late July. "We call this the 'flight to quality' to Indonesia, and based on my observation in the domestic capital markets, (Indonesian) government bonds have become been a safe haven for investors."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 How quickly things change. With markets spooked by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgrade of U.S. sovereign debt and Europe's debt crisis, $7 billion flowed out of Indonesia in August and September. Only about $900 million has returned in recent weeks despite an improvement in global market sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With such uncertainty, hedge funds are less concerned with the specific fundamentals of a given market and more concerned with lowering their overall risk profile, especially in emerging markets like Indonesia," said Keith Ducker, San Francisco-based chief investment officer at dark pool operator Tora.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts note the August and September outflows were less than half of the $15.2 billion in inflows to Indonesia in 2010, meaning there's still more money that could leave. Bank Indonesia's surprise rate cut Oct. 11 could further diminish the country's appeal to investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even Jakarta's strengths have a downside: As a major exporter of coal and crude palm oil, "Indonesia would suffer from any further fall in commodity prices" as global growth slows, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  global emerging markets strategist Geoffrey Dennis said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia officials say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has enough tools, including frequent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, to stabilize financial markets. Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also has around $114.5 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves it can use to stabilize asset prices, while the government has a IDR240 trillion fund to boost bond prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And at a sluggish moment globally, Indonesia's growth -- expected at 6.6% this year and 6.5% in 2012 -- sticks out.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think investors' confidence in us will not be easily shaken by any negative (global) sentiment," Waluyanto said in his July interview, "because if they pull out in big amounts, they don't know where else to invest their money."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By I Made Sentana; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 39831277; i-made.sentana@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Nisha Gopalan contributes to this article\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 24, 2011 03:25 ET (07:25 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111024e7ao001ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
