\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 912 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2011\par 09:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EFSF TALKS FOCUS ON COLLATERAL FOR GUARANTEES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European officials debating ways to increase the effectiveness of their bailout fund are focusing on using the fund to provide collateral to back up bond issues by troubled countries, according to people familiar with the matter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ SHIRAKAWA: BANK STANDS READY TO TAKE APPROPRIATE ACTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa reiterated Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stands ready to take further monetary policy steps if the economy deteriorates, while committing to keep a virtually zero interest rate policy until price stability is in sight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: STABILIZING PRICES STILL TOP MACRO-CONTROL GOAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stabilizing prices will remain China's top priority in guiding the macroeconomy, as the inflation rate is still high though price increases have slowed, People's Bank of China Assistant Gov. Jin Qi said in a speech posted on the PBOC's website Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH BANK FEDERATION SAYS BANKS STRONGER THAN EVER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of the French banking federation Frederic Oudea Thursday said French banks are stronger than ever and it is clear they are not asking for public funds as they continue strengthening their capital ratios.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SOME JAPAN REGIONS FEEL IMPACT OF SLOWING OVERSEAS ECON \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Five of nine regions in Japan upgraded their assessments of local economic conditions in October compared with three months earlier, although impact of slowing overseas economies has already been felt in some areas, a quarterly report released by the Bank of Japan showed Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP:GERMAN SEP PPI SLIGHTLY HIGHER ON MONTH THAN FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices at the factory gate rose slightly more than expected in September in monthly terms, Germany's federal statistics office, Destatis said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IRELAND MEETS LATEST BAILOUT TARGETS - FINANCE MINISTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ireland has met the latest targets of its bailout program, Finance Minister Michael Noonan said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU BANK RECAPITALIZATION COULD BE UNDER EUR100 BILLION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Union estimates that the region's banks have a capital shortfall of less than EUR100 billion ($137.46 billion) that must be made up over the next six to nine months, a figure well short of current market expectations, the Financial Times reported Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC CUTS BOND YIELD, FINE-TUNES MONETARY CONDITIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lowered the yield on a benchmark bond in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday for the first time since mid-August, sending a signal that Beijing has started easing monetary conditions to bolster a slowing economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA PRIME MINISTER: AIM TO KEEP 2011 INFLATION AS CLOSE TO 4% AS POSSIBLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Prime Minister Kim Hwang-sik Thursday said the government will do its best to keep 2011 inflation as close to its 4.0% target as possible, suggesting local officials expect inflation to come in above that level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA REPEATS VOW TO CURB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VOLATILITY; CONFIRMS MARKET \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Thursday reiterated that the government will act to curb excessive exchange-rate volatility when necessary, and acknowledged that the local authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market recently when the U.S. dollar rose above KRW1,200.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU COULD BAR RATINGS FIRMS FROM ISSUING REPORTS ON MEMBERS IN CRISIS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union Internal Market Commissioner Michel Barnier wants to bar ratings firms from publishing reports on members of the EU when they are facing crises, German daily Financial Times Deutschland reported Thursday, citing a draft document.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA TREASURY HEAD WARNS EU FAILURE WOULD ADD TO ECONOMIC RISKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasury Secretary and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Martin Parkinson said Thursday failure of euro-zone policy makers to resolve the region's sovereign debt crisis would add significantly to risks faced by the Australian economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA TIGHTENS RULES ON SUB-DEBT ISSUES FOR INSURERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's insurance regulator has toughened rules for insurers to raise funds from subordinated debt issues, in a move to strengthen its oversight of systemic risk in the financial sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDONESIA 3Q DOMESTIC, FOREIGN INVESTMENT UP 15.3% ON YEAR AT IDR65.4 TLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's official investment board said Thursday domestic and foreign companies spent IDR65.4 trillion on projects in Indonesia in the third quarter, or 15.3% more than in the same period a year earlier, despite mounting concerns about the health of the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK INDONESIA OFFICIAL: COMMITTED TO 'STAYING IN THE MARKET' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help stabilize the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial markets, a central banker wrote in an article published in the Jakarta Post Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SAYS POLICY STANCE APPROPRIATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely leave its monetary policy unchanged when it meets later today, with its governor noting that the current stance is appropriate to help spur economic activity amid global uncertainty and easing inflation worries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par BOJ WATCH: WEAK CPI GROWTH FORECAST SIGNALS NO TIGHTENING UNTIL SPRING 2014 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan may stick to its ultra-easy monetary policy for as long as until the spring of 2014, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to soon release a forecast indicating that the economy will remain in deflation for the next two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: HONG KONG INFLATION LIKELY EDGED HIGHER IN SEPTEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's consumer price inflation likely edged higher in September due to a steeper hike in private rentals amid a buoyant property market and a rise in food prices, economists said, adding that prices will likely peak in early 2012. [ 20-10-11 0753GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111020e7ak000n3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
