GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA REPEATS VOW TO CURB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VOLATILITY; CONFIRMS MARKET \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Thursday reiterated that the government will act to curb excessive exchange-rate volatility when necessary, and acknowledged that the local authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market recently when the U.S. dollar rose above KRW1,200. (published 0331 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EU COULD BAR RATINGS FIRMS FROM ISSUING REPORTS ON MEMBERS IN CRISIS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union Internal Market Commissioner Michel Barnier wants to bar ratings firms from publishing reports on members of the EU when they are facing crises, German daily Financial Times Deutschland reported Thursday, citing a draft document. (published 0320 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIA TREASURY HEAD WARNS EU FAILURE WOULD ADD TO ECONOMIC RISKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasury Secretary and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Martin Parkinson said Thursday failure of euro-zone policy makers to resolve the region's sovereign debt crisis would add significantly to risks faced by the Australian economy. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA TIGHTENS RULES ON SUB-DEBT ISSUES FOR INSURERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's insurance regulator has toughened rules for insurers to raise funds from subordinated debt issues, in a move to strengthen its oversight of systemic risk in the financial sector. (published 0329 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM PRIME MINISTER: 2011 GDP GROWTH LIKELY 6% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's gross domestic product is expected to expand 6% this year, Prime Minister Nguyen Tan Dung said Thursday. (published 0303 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: INDONESIA 3Q DOMESTIC, FOREIGN INVESTMENT UP 15.3% ON YEAR AT IDR65.4 TLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's official investment board said Thursday domestic and foreign companies spent IDR65.4 trillion on projects in Indonesia in the third quarter, or 15.3% more than in the same period a year earlier, despite mounting concerns about the health of the global economy. (published 0454 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA OFFICIAL: COMMITTED TO 'STAYING IN THE MARKET' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help stabilize the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial markets, a central banker wrote in an article published in the Jakarta Post Thursday. (published 0045 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SAYS POLICY STANCE APPROPRIATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely leave its monetary policy unchanged when it meets later today, with its governor noting that the current stance is appropriate to help spur economic activity amid global uncertainty and easing inflation worries. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA APPROVES BAOSTEEL TO SELL CNY6.5 BLN DIM SUM BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's top economic planning agency Thursday approved state-owned steel maker Baosteel Group Corp.'s plan to sell CNY6.5 billion of yuan-denominated bonds in Hong Kong, which will be the largest so-called dim sum bond issuance so far with the exception of offerings from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0551 GMT)