\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 837 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2011\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ SHIRAKAWA: BANK STANDS READY TO TAKE APPROPRIATE ACTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa reiterated Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stands ready to take further monetary policy steps if the economy deteriorates, while committing to keep a virtually zero interest rate policy until price stability is in sight. (published 0039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: STABILIZING PRICES STILL TOP MACRO-CONTROL GOAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stabilizing prices will remain China's top priority in guiding the macroeconomy, as the inflation rate is still high though price increases have slowed, People's Bank of China Assistant Gov. Jin Qi said in a speech posted on the PBOC's website Thursday. (published 0231 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: PBOC CUTS BOND YIELD, FINE-TUNES MONETARY CONDITIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lowered the yield on a benchmark bond in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday for the first time since mid-August, sending a signal that Beijing has started easing monetary conditions to bolster a slowing economy. (published 0439 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA PRIME MINISTER: AIM TO KEEP 2011 INFLATION AS CLOSE TO 4% AS POSSIBLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Prime Minister Kim Hwang-sik Thursday said the government will do its best to keep 2011 inflation as close to its 4.0% target as possible, suggesting local officials expect inflation to come in above that level. (published 0208 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA REPEATS VOW TO CURB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VOLATILITY; CONFIRMS MARKET \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Thursday reiterated that the government will act to curb excessive exchange-rate volatility when necessary, and acknowledged that the local authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market recently when the U.S. dollar rose above KRW1,200. (published 0331 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EU COULD BAR RATINGS FIRMS FROM ISSUING REPORTS ON MEMBERS IN CRISIS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union Internal Market Commissioner Michel Barnier wants to bar ratings firms from publishing reports on members of the EU when they are facing crises, German daily Financial Times Deutschland reported Thursday, citing a draft document. (published 0320 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIA TREASURY HEAD WARNS EU FAILURE WOULD ADD TO ECONOMIC RISKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian Treasury Secretary and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board member Martin Parkinson said Thursday failure of euro-zone policy makers to resolve the region's sovereign debt crisis would add significantly to risks faced by the Australian economy. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA TIGHTENS RULES ON SUB-DEBT ISSUES FOR INSURERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's insurance regulator has toughened rules for insurers to raise funds from subordinated debt issues, in a move to strengthen its oversight of systemic risk in the financial sector. (published 0329 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM PRIME MINISTER: 2011 GDP GROWTH LIKELY 6% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam's gross domestic product is expected to expand 6% this year, Prime Minister Nguyen Tan Dung said Thursday. (published 0303 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: INDONESIA 3Q DOMESTIC, FOREIGN INVESTMENT UP 15.3% ON YEAR AT IDR65.4 TLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's official investment board said Thursday domestic and foreign companies spent IDR65.4 trillion on projects in Indonesia in the third quarter, or 15.3% more than in the same period a year earlier, despite mounting concerns about the health of the global economy. (published 0454 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA OFFICIAL: COMMITTED TO 'STAYING IN THE MARKET' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help stabilize the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial markets, a central banker wrote in an article published in the Jakarta Post Thursday. (published 0045 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SAYS POLICY STANCE APPROPRIATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippines \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely leave its monetary policy unchanged when it meets later today, with its governor noting that the current stance is appropriate to help spur economic activity amid global uncertainty and easing inflation worries. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA ECONOMIC PLANNER APPROVES BAOSTEEL GROUP TO SELL CNY6.5 BLN DIM SUM BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's top economic planning agency, the National Development and Reform Commission, said Thursday it approved state-owned steel maker Baosteel Group Corp.'s plan to sell CNY6.5 billion worth of yuan-denominated bonds in Hong Kong. (published 0207 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PETROCHINA PARENT PRICES CNY3B TWO-TRANCHE OFFSHORE YUAN BOND - TERM SHEET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State-owned \f28 \uc2 \f2 China National Petroleum Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . has priced a CNY3 billion two-tranche offshore yuan bond in line with final guidance, according to a term sheet for the deal seen by Dow Jones Newswires Thursday. (published 0114 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: KNOC PRICES $1 BLN DOLLAR BOND; COULD SUPPORT MARKET REOPENING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State-run Korea National Oil Corp. has priced a $1 billion five-year dollar bond at 99.387 to yield 310 basis points over comparable Treasurys, in a deal that could encourage other Asian firms to issue U.S. dollar bonds. (published 0334 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: HONG KONG INFLATION LIKELY EDGED HIGHER IN SEPTEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's consumer price inflation likely edged higher in September due to a steeper hike in private rentals amid a buoyant property market and a rise in food prices, economists said, adding that prices will likely peak in early 2012. (published 0438 GMT) [ 20-10-11 0500GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111020e7ak0008v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
